WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.693
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.693 --> 00:00:09.852
Today, I'd like to talk to you about
what I think is the most interesting,

00:00:09.852 --> 00:00:14.490
the most exciting and the most
fascinating industry in the world.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.760
Now, it's been flying under the radar for
a little while, but

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.810
it's starting to attract
some really big headlines,

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.360
trillions of dollars in investment, and
it's projected for explosive growth.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.210
Now is really the time to get involved.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.280
I think it's going to
define the next 50 years.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.634
What is this miracle industry?

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:33.675
Government!

00:00:33.675 --> 00:00:37.585
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yay.

00:00:37.585 --> 00:00:40.744
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I know what you're thinking,

00:00:40.744 --> 00:00:43.430
I slightly tricked you there because
government is not cool or sexy.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.300
Well, I think it is.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.310
I think it's fascinating,

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.610
and I'm interested in how we do
government differently and better.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.700
In particular, I'm interested in how we
take the experiences of the people who

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.990
really need government and build them into
the way we design government services.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.070
Now that's not a new idea.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.690
Abraham Lincoln was talking about
government by the people, of the people,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.420
for the people, way back when.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.500
But having spent ten years working in and
around government,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.690
it's my experience that we've
drifted quite a long way from that.

00:01:16.386 --> 00:01:21.022
So, for us, the kind of interactions that
we have with government tend to be things

00:01:21.022 --> 00:01:25.791
like going to the DMV, or registering with
the IRS, or going through TSA processes,

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:27.780
or perhaps US immigration.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.660
And I'm willing to bet that these
are not always the most seamless,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.660
enjoyable experiences you've ever had.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:36.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But imagine how much worse that is if

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:42.400
you're 85, or if you can't read,
or if you're severely depressed.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.550
Then having a difficult experience with
government isn't just a frustration,

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.460
it's a real barrier that locks you
our of the service and into poverty.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.700
Now, I had a vivid illustration
of this when I was volunteering

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.520
in Detroit on a program to redesign
the process for applying for food stamps.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.103
I was in a rather damp and
dingy community center,

00:02:06.103 --> 00:02:10.480
and the strip lights flickering
overhead in the 8 mile part of Detroit.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.810
And I was with a young lady called Monica,
and she was trying to go in to apply for

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:15.390
food stamps.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:22.064
Now, the process begins with a 42 page
long form of mind-bending complexity,

00:02:22.064 --> 00:02:24.287
everything about font size one.

00:02:24.287 --> 00:02:30.039
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And it looks like this.

00:02:32.466 --> 00:02:36.631
Monica was in the fourth hour of
trying to fill in this form, and

00:02:36.631 --> 00:02:39.460
she just reached the question which says,

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:44.430
declare all your liquid assets,
failure to do so will result in a penalty.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.658
And she looks at me horror stricken,
and she said, well,

00:02:51.658 --> 00:02:57.225
liquid assets,
I've got some milk in the fridge.

00:02:57.225 --> 00:03:00.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That was funny, right?

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.920
I mean it's funny to
us because we're safe.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.350
Our lives are great, but for Monica if
she didn't fill in this form correctly,

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.700
that meant she couldn't feed her children.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.890
Now it's not just a problem for the people
who use government services if they're not

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.170
well designed, it's also a problem for
the people who are trying to improve them.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.540
I had a forceful illustration of
this when I was working to try and

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.660
cut crime in West London.

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.399
We'd taken the unusual step of going
to spend eight days living with

00:03:29.399 --> 00:03:31.252
a group of addicts who were in and
out of prison.

00:03:33.265 --> 00:03:35.130
This was a really amazing experience for
me.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.640
One of the things that stood out to me
most clearly was their attitude to prison.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.770
So, I assume prison's bad, right?

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:42.270
Prison's bad.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.510
Well, they actually seem to quite like it,
and that was puzzling to me.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.930
In fact, when we surveyed them,

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:53.900
eight out of ten, said if I could
self-refer into prison, I would do that.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.470
As in, if I could tick a box saying,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.699
I'd like to go to prison now,
then that's what they would do.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.263
And I found this really extraordinary.

00:04:00.263 --> 00:04:04.081
[LAUGH] So, I said, one of the is
called Nick, and I said to him,

00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:08.430
could you explain a little bit more to me,
Nick, about how this works?

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.150
He said, yeah, it's pretty easy.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.820
It's basically, I like a combination of
freedom in my life, just like you do, but

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.550
I also like structure.

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:17.570
A prison gives me structure.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.670
So, when you're maybe having
a few too many donuts you think,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.740
I probably need a personal trainer now.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.520
Well for
me when I've had a few too many drugs,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.900
when I've been out on the razz too much,
when I'm nicked too much I think,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.540
I need to turn this around, and
prison is my personal trainer.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.615
[SOUND] This is like mind blowing, right?

00:04:33.615 --> 00:04:34.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So we're trying

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.900
to design a better service for these guys.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.490
And we're trying to work on what
would change their behavior.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:47.678
And we're so far off that what we think of
as the stick, they think is the carrot.

00:04:47.678 --> 00:04:52.261
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I would never have realized that if I

00:04:52.261 --> 00:04:57.824
hadn't got out of my ivory tower and
gone and spent real time with them.

00:04:57.824 --> 00:05:00.730
So, it's not surprising, really,
that government gets this stuff wrong.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:04.980
Because when you think about it,
the people who run government

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.630
usually have no direct personal experience
of the problem they're trying to solve.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.400
That's not to say that
they're not well meaning or

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.280
hugely intelligent, they're usually both.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.340
It's just to say that the people
who work on bad schools

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.439
usually went to great schools.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:26.450
The people who work on aging
policy are usually of working age.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.100
The people who work on
health policy are healthy.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:33.050
And in fact, if you want to work on prison
reform, it's an absolute requirement,

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:36.025
the first thing we check,
is that you've never been in prison.

00:05:36.025 --> 00:05:39.747
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:05:39.747 --> 00:05:45.330
what do we do instead?

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.180
Well, we rely on brains.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.260
It's good to be intelligent,
brains are helpful.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.230
But intelligence is a complement
to good government.

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.501
It's not a substitute for
direct experience of the problem.

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:02.430
Now, I know of what I speak
because I was one of these people.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.050
I was a technocrat.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.160
After a hot shot job in consulting,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.060
a great education,
I was going to serve the government.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.790
And so,
I was very excited when on my first day,

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:15.460
I got assigned to a team that
was working on homelessness.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.000
I thought, fantastic,
this is such an important issue.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.770
So, I said, when are we going to go and
interview some more homeless people?

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:27.010
And I was a bit surprised
when the team said, well,

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.840
I don't really know about that,
because we've read a lot of research,

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.770
we talked to some academics, and
we've also been speaking to the people in

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.110
the homeless charities who do
the lobbying on this sort of stuff.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.200
All right, well, that's great then,
right, brilliant.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:43.610
But I don't think so actually because
I used to, I know some of these guys.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.000
In fact I was at Cambridge
with them reading classics.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.065
They're nice people.

00:06:47.065 --> 00:06:48.660
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.150
they definitely don't have direct
personal experience of homelessness.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.800
The real experts on homelessness
are homeless people.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:56.500
We weren't going to be
speaking to any of them.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.360
It's no good being the smartest
person in the room

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.260
if you never actually go into a room
with the people you're trying to help.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.230
So this moment sent me searching for
a better way of doing government.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:14.730
And I'm pleased that I found one and it's
actually right here in Silicon Valley.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.770
It's to apply the techniques of design
thinking, of human-centered design,

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:22.800
which we use in the lean startup
methodology here, into government.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:29.253
It's not too complicated, basically it's
spending time with the people who you're

00:07:29.253 --> 00:07:33.040
trying to help and then co-creating
something better with them.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:38.250
Now when I say design, you're probably
thinking of something like this,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.409
or this, and that's design.

00:07:40.409 --> 00:07:42.600
This is design too.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.690
What makes Airbnb work is not
the pretty colors, it is that they

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:50.950
thought really carefully about what would
make for a great user experience for you,

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.850
and they've built something
seamless around you.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:56.880
Government should be the same.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.910
So when you do this,
you get much better results.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.990
First, you create a service
which actually works.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.640
I'm sorry to say that a lot of
government services don't really work.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.690
They don't solve the problem that
they set out to try and solve, and

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:13.540
people cycle in and
out of them over many years.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.590
Secondly, it's usually a lot cheaper.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.140
You spend time looking carefully at
what service people actually want.

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:22.759
You can usually cut out a lot
of stuff they didn't want and

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:23.687
didn't use in the first place.

00:08:25.102 --> 00:08:27.690
Thirdly, this is inspiring work.

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:32.360
Because design connects you directly back
to the people you came into public service

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.912
to help,
you get past all that pen-pushing and

00:08:34.912 --> 00:08:37.040
form-filling which we
associate with government.

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:39.500
It really connects you to what
you came into public service for

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.194
in the first place.

00:08:40.194 --> 00:08:46.381
And finally, it's respectful.

00:08:48.466 --> 00:08:49.190
This is not respectful.

00:08:52.662 --> 00:08:56.430
So don't give in to the cynicism
about what government can do,

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.702
it works on crucial topics
like ending child poverty,

00:08:59.702 --> 00:09:03.630
like cutting domestic violence,
by building a fairer society.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:07.990
And it does so with a huge ton of cash.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:11.070
We are not talking about 1% of your
corporate profits here, we are talking

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:15.000
about really channeling trillions
of dollars to things that matter.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.659
If that's not inspiring,
I don't know what is.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.490
So what can you do?

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:24.970
Well, if you work in government,
then I really encourage you

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.950
to take the time to find out a little bit
more about human-centered design, and

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:30.245
see if you can apply it to your work.

00:09:30.245 --> 00:09:34.510
It doesn't have to be rocket science, it's
just about going and spending some time

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:38.570
with the people that you're trying to
help, in their office, not in yours.

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:43.868
And if you don't work in government,
well, there's still something you can do,

00:09:43.868 --> 00:09:48.825
stop trash-talking government.

00:09:48.825 --> 00:09:52.612
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Is it perfect?

00:09:52.612 --> 00:09:55.390
No.
Could it be better?

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:55.890
Yes.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.170
And would it be a lot better if
people who were entrepreneurial,

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:02.100
who know about design, who know about
lean startup, who are bright and

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.550
talented, people like the people
in this room, got involved?

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:07.230
Absolutely.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:15.278
So, if you want to make your millions in
the private sector, great, this is good.

00:10:15.278 --> 00:10:17.874
But if you want to work on
something that really matters,

00:10:17.874 --> 00:10:19.819
if you want to get up
each day with purpose,

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:24.080
if you want to build a fairer society, if
you want to do the work you were born for.

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.220
Then I encourage you to join me
in this fantastic joint endeavor

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:33.063
we call government, and together we
can build government by the people,

00:10:33.063 --> 00:10:35.347
with the people, for the people.

00:10:35.347 --> 00:10:36.379
Thank you.

00:10:36.379 --> 00:10:40.420
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:43.764
[MUSIC]

